WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:08.250
NANDEN SRIDHAR:
My name is Nanden.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.020
I'm a PM with Apigee.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.330
And I'm going to be
talking to guys a bit

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.720
about API management and
microservices before I

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.573
introduce Gallo from HP.

00:00:17.573 --> 00:00:18.990
And he's going to
talk to you guys

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.700
about how HP used microservices
and API management

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.690
in their transformation.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.550
So let's start off very
quickly with why enterprises

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.100
are building microservices-based
architectures

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.830
and what's the driving factor?

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.730
Two main reasons come to my
mind when enterprises are

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.500
talking about microservices.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.570
The first is that they
want to take advantage

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.720
of cloud-based infrastructures.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.590
Now, we may all not agree
on what microservices are.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.950
But there's general consensus
on what a microservice's stack

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.240
looks like.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.280
And the general idea is that
by building your applications

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.580
or workloads to be compatible
with this architecture enables

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.620
you to move these
workloads to the cloud.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.360
Even if they don't
move right away,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.330
gives the companies choices
to move to the cloud.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.690
The second big idea
behind microservices--

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.250
and we hear this all the time--

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.390
is velocity, speed,
agility, and all of this.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.030
Right?

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.480
And the problem with
monolith applications

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.940
was it took forever to
test it and it took forever

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.830
to make production releases.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.440
And microservices
promised an architecture

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.530
where you had the ability
to make deployments faster.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.900
So as enterprises started
decomposing their microservices

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.950
into independent
units, developers

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.650
got the chance to independently
develop, deploy, version, scale

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.840
those services.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.570
And these are problems that you
didn't have with microservices.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.850
So in theory, we should
come to the conclusion

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.040
that yes, microservices
are awesome.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.020
All problems were solved.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.890
While it is true that
the challenges with speed

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.020
and agility were
definitely solved

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.480
with a microservices
architecture,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.980
new problems were introduced.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:08.530
Right?

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.080
When you had all
these development

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.010
teams building microservices,
governance became a big issue.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.660
Where do I do MTLS?

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.370
Where do I do a load balancing?

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.400
How do I ensure that the
right telemetry is collected?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.260
Or there's a performance issue.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.510
Every microservices
team says yep,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.980
my service is doing just fine.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.650
And so we saw the advent of
service management tools.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.330
And so a service
management tool is

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.990
really catered to the persona
of a service operator.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.760
A service operator
is someone who's

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.770
concerned with the
fleet of services

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.330
that he or she manages.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.790
And they are entrusted
to apply governance

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.140
across these services.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.370
That governance standard could
be MTLS for all my services,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.320
I want to capture telemetry--

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.460
not have every microservices
team invent these concepts

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.170
for their microservices nor have
these things put into the code

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.850
base of your microservices.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.970
Start to think of these
capabilities as available

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.050
in the infrastructure itself.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.980
So I get features like
governance, observability,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.300
and basic security into
each of my microservices.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.850
So imagine that you
have microservices

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:22.650
and you're using a tool that
gives you these capabilities.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.580
Is it done?

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.740
Is there anything
else that is missing?

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.830
Well, when you think
about it, yes, we

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.150
have solved the problem
for the service operator

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.380
who's looking at these
services in production.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.840
But before services
get to production,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.690
there is a set of
capabilities that we need

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.150
for producers and consumers.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.180
So teams that are
building microservices

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.130
are often building
microservices that

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.140
are consumed by other teams--

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.300
teams outside your
zone of trust.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.910
Those other teams could
be in other departments

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.300
in your organization, could
even be outside the enterprise.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.600
So when you have teams
outside your enterprise

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.270
who are consuming
services, where

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.070
do they find your microservices?

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.020
How do they know what
the documentation

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:08.910
for your microservices
looks like?

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.100
How do they get access
to these services?

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.320
And how do you manage that?

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:17.100
So the concept of managing the
relationship between producers

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.350
and consumers is critical
to API management.

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.050
So a consumer of
your microservice

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.660
should go to a
portal, seek access

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.820
to a microservice he
or she wants access

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.190
to because they're building
other microservices based

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.900
on this.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.870
And now comes the
concerns of a provider.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.660
As a provider, I
want to manage who

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.610
can access my microservices.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.060
I want to be able to revoke
fair access or grant access.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.460
If I am granting
access, I may want

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.000
to grant different degrees of
access to my microservices.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.240
I'll build a microservice once
and have different degrees

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.650
of access to internal consumers
versus external consumers.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.740
So at one point,
yes, as a developer--

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.170
as a producer, I want
to engage consumers,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.950
I want to control that access,
and then also control what type

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.360
of access I want to give them.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.010
So once you've defined who
gets access to my microservices

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.950
and how they get access to
it, the next step, obviously,

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.710
is to enforce that access--

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.480
policy enforcement.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.520
When we think of
policy enforcement,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.210
we really think about a gateway.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.520
A gateway it's
typically a place that's

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.810
where you do policy
enforcement, also

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.360
known as policy
enforcement points.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.340
I mean, when you think
about API gateways,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.130
there is a picture that
comes to mind or capabilities

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.040
that we all expect
API gateways to have.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.050
You want your API
gateways to do things

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.640
like rate limitings
or [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.350
You want those gateways
to verify tokens.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.090
You want the gateways
to even provide

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.670
other features not
related to security,

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.530
like caching of
requests or responses.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:57.720
These are all things that we've
come to expect of a gateway.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.700
And when we talk
about API gateways,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.160
a pattern that has
served for many years

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.880
is this sort of a
centralized API gateway.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.210
These cluster or
groups of APIs gateways

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:15.570
often serve as a single
entry point into your APIs.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.750
API-- your company name-- would
.com would really be the one

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.810
that's receiving the requests,
which is a group of gateways

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.020
that are a facade
to all your APIs.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.000
And these gateways did
functions like rate

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.480
limiting, checking the token--
did all this in addition

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.100
to more advanced features, like
aggregating a bunch of services

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.320
to give you the facade
as if they were one.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.040
They also orchestrated other
microservices or services.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.470
And they performed that
function fairly well.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.190
But when you start
thinking about using

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.760
this pattern for microservices,
something doesn't always fit.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.080
Right?

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.390
It is not uncommon for
a single transaction

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.790
from one of your
channels to a back-end go

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.410
through a number
of microservices

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.260
before it goes to the database.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.260
And as it goes through
your microservices,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.200
it is onerous notice to have
every microservice go back

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.080
to a centralized gateway
and hit the database.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.140
Clearly that's not
a viable option.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.390
And so, to deal
with this situation,

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:20.040
we now see patterns that
are sidecar API gateways.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.130
Sometimes that also referred
to as microgateways.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.780
So a sidecar is a process
that runs in the same trust

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.760
zone as your microservice.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.500
You really have
to think about it

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.620
as an extension of
your application.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:34.620
It's not something else.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.810
But it's a separate process.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.870
And the application of the
microservice in this case

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.020
delegates certain
responsibilities to the sidecar

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.810
so you don't have to
implement it yourself.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.650
And a sidecar gateway is
ideal for implementing

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.080
allow-deny type of rules.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.210
What are allow-deny
type of rules?

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.600
The rate limiting is one-- do
I allow traffic, to block it?

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.570
Checking a token or a
key is a allow-deny rule.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.030
Do I allow this
access or deny this?

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.670
And they're perfect for this
sort of a scenario, which

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:09.030
is typically what we see when
you have microservices calling

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:11.640
other microservices.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.760
So does that mean that
that's the only pattern that

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.210
fits microservices, that
everything is a sidecar

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:19.960
gateway?

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.590
But our position is that
you don't necessarily

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.270
have to choose.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.840
You could have an
intelligent ingress

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:30.050
that is able to
decide for every API,

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.440
should this API be
enforced by a sidecar

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.559
or should this be enforced by
a more centralized gateway?

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:38.390
And you could use
various techniques

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.760
to discriminate what
type of a gateway

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.409
needs to enforce
policies on this.

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:46.320
So based on a verb, a path,
something in the header,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.620
you could route traffic
to a centralized gateway

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.710
to get more advanced functions
for it because that API needs

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.500
it, or it could be directly
routed to a sidecar

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.180
because that is all
what was needed.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.000
And there will be some
cases which really

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.050
go through both gateways.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:01.910
And that's perfectly
fine, too, as long

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:05.270
as you're not duplicating
functionality.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.320
So the other thing
to remember as you're

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.480
building microservices
is that not everything

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.180
in your enterprise is
going to be a microservice.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.430
Yeah, sure, you're all
in the transition phase

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.650
where you're building
applications and that.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:20.770
But there are also other
types of applications

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.490
in your enterprise.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.580
Most enterprises will
still have a good set

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.350
of SOA-based services that are
trying to modernize slowly.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:32.680
Enterprises are being forced
to expose a lot of their APIs

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:33.570
to the public--

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.620
you know, standards
like open banking

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.590
are forcing banks to have
public-facing APIs, things

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:40.725
that didn't happen
three, four years ago.

00:09:40.725 --> 00:09:42.100
So as an enterprise,
you're going

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:45.250
to have a combination of
all these requirements.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.440
Also not uncommon
is for enterprises

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.010
to invoke APIs built
by other vendors--

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.590
Salesforce, Workday,
these are all APIs

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.030
that you want to make available.

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:59.140
It's not really nice to have
your microservices developer

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.760
get another OAR
token for Salesforce,

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:05.350
get an OAR token internally
and have to manage all this.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.470
It'd be really nice to have
some sort of token mediation

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:11.080
so your microservices
developer deals with one

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.910
type of an access
token that gets

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:16.100
mediated between different
types of standards.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:21.370
So our recommendation is that
when building microservices,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.860
use a service management tool.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:27.040
Most service management
tools, like Istio or Linkerd,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.410
they come with a sidecar.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.780
And as much as possible,
use the sidecar

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.030
to act as your
sidecar API gateway.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.910
So in the case of Istio,
one way would be that sort

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:39.370
of a sidecar API gateway.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.370
And for cases where do you need
a more centralized gateway,

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:45.370
think about a centralized
API management

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.820
platform that is able to
offer these capabilities.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:54.310
So Apigee has a gateway
that gives you capabilities

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.110
for all these form factors.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.850
We have the
enterprise API gateway

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.442
that we have now had for
about six or seven years.

00:11:00.442 --> 00:11:01.900
And it's been
running in production

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.770
with many of our customers.

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.040
But we have also introduced
two types of gateways--

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.850
the microgateway for customers
who have not yet adopted

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:12.480
a service mesh like Istio.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.880
And we have built the
Apigee adapter for Istio

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:17.680
for enterprises that
have started to adopt

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.110
Istio for service management.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:24.160
The key point here is that the
API management platform becomes

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:27.340
a central control plane
through which you can control

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:29.470
different classes of
gateways depending

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:32.090
on the needs of your enterprise.

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:34.210
So with this quick
introduction, I

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.520
invite Gallo now
to talk about how

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.340
HP use some of
these technologies

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.520
at their enterprise.

00:11:44.391 --> 00:11:47.190
GALLO GIMENEZ-PALOP:
OK, thank you, Nanden.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.690
OK, so my name is Gallo Gimenez.

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:52.840
I work at HP at the
office of this CTO,

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:57.580
where we are basically building
infrastructure and core

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:01.930
services that support our
devices and our applications.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.030
I've been in HP for
more than 20 years now.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.795
And during those
years, almost 15 years

00:12:07.795 --> 00:12:09.670
I have basically been
in distributed systems.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:15.000
So I won't go into the numbers.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.130
HP was a company that
was found that in 1939.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:21.570
So it's very old company
for Silicon Valley.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.480
In 2015, the company
was reinvented to focus

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.480
on three areas--

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:28.290
one is basically HP
computing, so things like

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:30.327
workstations and Chromebooks.

00:12:30.327 --> 00:12:31.410
The other one is printing.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:35.370
So with all things from $29
printers to several million

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:36.300
dollar printers.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.460
And the last one is
3D manufacturing.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:40.410
One of the important
things that we have here

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:43.770
is that HP is operating
in 170 countries.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.190
So that means that
compliance and are

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.410
regulations had an important
aspect of everything

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:49.960
that we do.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:51.930
And I'm going to
basically talk how

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:57.480
we use the cloud and the service
meshes and practice to do that.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.060
So in HP, there is
like three trends

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.800
that are affecting
the way we do things.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.110
One is what we call One Life.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:06.390
One Life is basically
the blending

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.280
of our personal and
professional life.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:11.880
So we use the same devices
for the work and play.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.230
The other one is as-a-service.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:16.740
So the work to market is
changing from a transactional

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.060
to contractual.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.250
So you can see this
in IT organizations

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:24.080
of loading their operations
through other groups.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.790
So basically they don't have
to deploy, manage, and secure

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.290
services.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:31.540
And the last one is
security and privacy--

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.840
which is obviously with
more devices connected,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.900
consumers and
organizations are basically

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.260
consuming and producing
very valuable data

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:41.420
that we need to protect.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:42.750
OK?

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:45.690
So these trends basically
set the direction

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:46.692
of our organization.

00:13:46.692 --> 00:13:48.150
And then in our
organization, there

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.370
is basically two
groups that have

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:51.920
a big say in how we do cloud.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:53.130
One is IT.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.650
IT, with the change
in 2015, decided

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.890
not to focus anymore
on data centers,

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:00.310
and migrate everything
to the public cloud.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:05.070
And obviously IT traditionally
focuses on security and cost.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.980
My group, the CTO office,
basically traditionally we

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.890
focus on things
such as innovation.

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.200
We try to bring best
practices from the industry

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.230
into the tech communities in HP.

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:17.440
And then because we are
a neutral organization,

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.490
we took on the work of
doing these shared services

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.990
across other organizations.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:24.900
Finally, my group
is very focused

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.180
on privacy and
security, especially

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:31.470
for global applications because
all the markets where we are.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.940
So-- sorry, I need to go back.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.980
So this basically defines our
strategy for how we do cloud.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:44.400
I mean, there is nothing that
should surprise you here.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.350
So basically we
are a cloud native

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:48.760
because we are in
the public cloud.

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:51.810
We do Agile, we do continuous
delivery and integration.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:52.830
We do dev ops.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.600
And because we have
this shared services,

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.100
contracts are very
important for us.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.482
And for us, contracts are
basically APIs and SLAs.

00:14:59.482 --> 00:15:01.065
Security and privacy,
as I said, these

00:15:01.065 --> 00:15:04.330
are very important role for
both IT and the CT office.

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.690
And finally, microservices
is part of our strategy.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.190
And what I'm going
to explain to you

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.280
is how we do microservices
at HP and why we do it.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.570
OK?

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.120
So why we do microservices--
the real reason

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:17.760
is basically for
product velocity.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.853
We are building
these applications.

00:15:19.853 --> 00:15:21.270
Initially, these
applications will

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.840
be built by very large
teams with many people.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.090
And this required a
lot of synchronization

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.400
between those teams.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.530
These teams usually in
different functional areas

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:34.710
of the application will have
dependencies across them.

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:36.630
And sometimes the
dependencies were circular.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:38.760
So basically this required
even more meetings

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.890
to solve those dependencies.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.900
We had this continuous
integration pipelines

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.440
that would take hours.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.380
And because there were
so many dependencies,

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.690
there were manual gates
to decide if something was

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.310
moving or not moving forward.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.270
Right?

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:54.210
So, at the end, the
result was that features

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.250
will take very long,
much more longer

00:15:56.250 --> 00:16:00.330
than what all we wanted
to put into production.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.760
So with microservices,
what we were looking for

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:05.830
was to increase the
product velocity.

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:10.380
So we have a smaller code base
that our teams can handle.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:14.130
We tried to have those smart
applications and services go

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:15.930
into production
independently so they

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.580
don't have to have the
dependencies with other teams.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:21.750
We have a smaller teams so the
communication in those smaller

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:22.780
teams is less.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.030
So we have less communication.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:26.570
It's more efficient, because
we can have these smaller

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.573
teams usually co-located.

00:16:28.573 --> 00:16:30.240
And then because they
are smaller teams,

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.500
they can on the whole
application is done.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.350
So they can own
how the application

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.283
is developed, coded, tested,
moved into production.

00:16:37.283 --> 00:16:39.450
And they are usually operating
in those applications

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:39.950
themselves.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.282
So they are on pager
duty, they get the alerts.

00:16:42.282 --> 00:16:43.490
And they own the whole thing.

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:45.840
So we call it you
build it, you run it.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:52.320
But when we started to
do the microservices,

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.860
obviously microservices
are more complicated

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.220
that monolith applications.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.560
Microservices have lots
of technical problems.

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:02.940
So the first one we found
was infrastructure cost.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.130
Suddenly, we have
all these VMs, right?

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:06.720
And these VMs became
very expensive.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:08.250
Because we have
many of those VMs

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.680
for every single service
and different environments

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.210
for every service.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.486
We had dozens and dozens
of continuous integration

00:17:15.486 --> 00:17:16.319
on pipelines, right?

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:19.050
Because now you have
many, many, many services.

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:22.050
Orchestration and discovery
becomes way more complicated

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:24.160
because services depend
on other services.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:26.192
So you need to know where
the other service is

00:17:26.192 --> 00:17:27.900
as in which environment
are you running--

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.970
are you in production or in VA?

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:32.220
And basically, you have to
depend on the other service

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.610
to be there in order for your
service to start working.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.665
There is challenges that
are traditional and normal

00:17:38.665 --> 00:17:40.300
in these [INAUDIBLE] systems.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:42.120
You have latency variance.

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.160
And you have network partitions.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.740
You need to deal with
distributed transactions

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.030
across different services.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.970
So those are very hard
problems to solve sometimes.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.430
We have problems with
dependency versions.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:55.710
Now you have services that
depend on other services.

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.710
And those other services have
new versions continuously.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.600
So how do you know which
is the other version

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:02.850
of the other service and
when the other team changes

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.730
the version of the service?

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:09.240
Integration testing became
really, really difficult

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.880
because now, when you
release one of the services,

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.740
you need to do the
integration testing

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:17.200
with a bunch of other
testing other services.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:20.280
And finally, the problem
which I'm going to focus now

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:21.840
is basically policy spread.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.610
So this is an example
of an application

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:29.280
with several microservices.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:31.535
I call them A, B,
and C. And usually

00:18:31.535 --> 00:18:33.910
you are going to have that
you have more than one version

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:37.720
of those services-- so we call
them alpha, beta, gamma, and so

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:39.130
on.

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:40.870
And these services
may be managing

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.070
different types of data.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.420
Some of the data is personal.

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:47.520
Some of the data is data that
is not sensitive or doesn't

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.920
contain personal
information, and so on.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:53.110
And your mesh-- and you
might think that this

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:54.590
is a very complex application.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.825
But as soon as you start
to do microservices,

00:18:57.825 --> 00:18:59.200
you will find that
it's very easy

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:02.870
to get in a complicated
mess like this.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:04.870
Now, you will end up
deploying this application

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.420
in multiple data centers
or Kubernetes clusters,

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:08.680
whatever you deploy.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.970
And your mesh might even
get more complicated.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:14.470
So you will have, on
top of A, B, and C,

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:17.470
and alpha, beta, and gamma,
you are going to have A-prime

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:19.630
and alpha-prime, right?

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.800
And the decision of where
do you deploy things

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.610
in the different
data centers, it

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:27.130
might depend on cost
considerations or latency

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:28.480
considerations, right?

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:34.180
So this mesh is going to get
more messy and complicated.

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:36.670
So when you look
at the things you

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.345
need to do in all
of these services,

00:19:39.345 --> 00:19:41.720
you will see that you are
repeating things over and over.

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:46.780
So basically, you need to apply
things such as authorization,

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:49.330
authentication, you need
to do payload inspection,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:52.150
you need to do rates, you
need to do quotas, right?

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:55.960
And you do this over, and
over, and over, and over

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.757
in every single place.

00:19:57.757 --> 00:19:59.590
In the worst case, you
are going to be doing

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:01.120
this in different languages.

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.152
Because those services
might be implemented

00:20:03.152 --> 00:20:04.110
in different languages.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:05.570
Some might be note applications.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:06.830
Some might be Go applications.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:08.830
Some might be Java applications.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:12.430
So at the end, you have policy
spread all over the place.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:14.170
It is very difficult
to understand what

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:16.630
is your position about policy.

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:18.940
You don't have any
visibility about what

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:20.650
is going on in the system.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.210
And worse, it is very easy
to have a single mistake that

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.270
is going to screw
up-- basically is

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:30.630
going to bring your system
to a very bad situation.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:34.670
So the good news is
there is things that can

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.990
help to solve this problems.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:40.550
So one of the technologies
that helps our containers

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:42.920
in Kubernetes,
they basically help

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:46.400
us to have more density
in our deployments.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.300
They solve some of the
programs around orchestration

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:50.750
and discovery.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.910
The other one is API management.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:55.070
So now we can have
written contracts

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:58.980
with the consumers of our
APIs that we can version.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.000
And we can have visibility about
how our consumers are using

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.800
the APIs of different services.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:07.460
We have API gateways and
proxies that basically

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:10.070
allows that to centralize
some of the policies,

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:11.300
as Nanden described before.

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:13.730
So we can have things
such as authorization

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:16.850
and authentication centralized
in a single location,

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:18.752
or payload inspection.

00:21:18.752 --> 00:21:20.210
And finally, with
the service mesh,

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:23.030
we can go further and have
additional network policies

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:24.560
implemented by the mesh.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:27.290
So we can have things such
as traffic encryption,

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:29.880
or basically balancing
between services, and so on.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:30.700
OK?

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:34.740
So I'm going to focus
on the last ones.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.773
So in HP, we have different
applications, as everybody.

00:21:37.773 --> 00:21:39.190
So we have everything
from latency

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:41.030
to very new application.

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.920
And we have these
three different models,

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:46.590
as Nanden described it,
about how we do these things.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:48.850
So the first thing is we
have a monolith applications.

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:51.080
These applications,
sometimes they

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.260
are deployed in
governance clusters.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:55.760
Sometimes they are
deployed in VMs, right?

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:58.340
But we still need to have this
common control plane where

00:21:58.340 --> 00:21:59.510
we want to put the policies.

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:02.150
In this case, we use
this centralized proxy

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:03.770
that basically
takes the policies

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:06.740
such as authentication,
authorization, and rate limits,

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.420
and that's the
location where we are.

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:10.628
In the case where we have
this important coordinators

00:22:10.628 --> 00:22:12.020
or in the case
where we have VMs,

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:15.180
there is no difference, right?

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:18.000
Newer applications that we
started a few years ago,

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.760
we started to use the
sidecar container paradigm.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:22.970
So basically, we
embed microgateway

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:24.187
in front of our services.

00:22:24.187 --> 00:22:26.270
So basically, our continuous
integration pipeline,

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.430
when you deploy one
of those services,

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:31.300
injects a safeguard
container into the box.

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.150
And that sidecar
container, in our case--

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.480
we base it on APG's
microgateway--

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:37.830
it's connected to the
APG control plane.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:39.907
So we can manage some
of the policies there.

00:22:39.907 --> 00:22:41.990
Now, many of the policies
we cannot manage through

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:42.810
the control plane.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:45.738
They need to be embedded
into the microgateway itself.

00:22:45.738 --> 00:22:47.780
So basically, our continuous
integration pipeline

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:49.460
configuration
management mechanism

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:51.740
is making sure we have
the right policies there.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:53.690
Still, this is a
little bit problematic

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:56.090
because basically the
microgateway for service A

00:22:56.090 --> 00:22:59.411
and the microgateway for service
B might be different, right?

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:05.460
So the last services
that we are building,

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:07.500
we are leveraging Istio.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.570
The difference with
the previous model I

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:12.300
showed you before is now that
you have this common control

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:16.230
plane when you can manage a very
large freight of microgateways

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:17.345
or proxies.

00:23:17.345 --> 00:23:19.720
So you have all their policies
managed in a single place.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.240
So you don't need to
have different versions

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.520
of these microgateway for
the different services.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.670
You can manage this centrally.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:27.600
Another interesting
thing about Istio,

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:30.390
it provides just not
policies on the ingress,

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.920
but you can have policies
also in the egress.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:36.000
So you can control to whom
your services are talking.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.760
Or you can white-list or
blacklist some services

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:40.580
where you don't want
the traffic to go.

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:41.080
OK?

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:46.560
So I'm going to show you
a couple of applications

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:49.720
where do use this.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:51.600
So this is one of the
largest applications

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.130
that we have right now.

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:53.940
It's HP-ID.

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:57.060
Basically, HP-ID is the HP
Identity Management system.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.780
So if you hold the HP.com
you have a printer that

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:03.120
is connected to the
cloud, your identity

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:05.010
is managed by this system.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:09.120
This application right now is
running in three data centers.

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:11.100
Basically we have three regions.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:14.190
And we have coordinated
cluster in every region.

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:16.770
In this case, we are using
the microgateway model.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.550
You can see these
orange bubbles there.

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:20.340
That's the microgateway
that we inject

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.320
in front of the services.

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:24.960
And the traffic is sent to the
clusters through an ingress.

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:27.630
In this case, we
are using Nginx.

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:31.380
You will notice that
some of our bots

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:32.880
have just the microgateway.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:36.240
And the reason is we have
some back-end services that

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:37.320
are still run on VMs.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.100
They are not in this diagram.

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:42.433
And we also proxy the
traffic to those services

00:24:42.433 --> 00:24:43.350
through microgateways.

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:46.355
So basically, we apply
a consistent way of-- we

00:24:46.355 --> 00:24:47.730
have a consistent
way of applying

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:50.820
this policies to everything that
we have in our infrastructure.

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:53.790
In this case, we decided to put
the microgateway in Kubernetes

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:56.430
because it's, for us,
far easier to scale,

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:59.220
to manage, and to release than
having those microgateways

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:00.917
deployed in VMs with
the services that

00:25:00.917 --> 00:25:01.750
are in the back-end.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:02.250
OK?

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.400
This is another application.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:08.920
This is our content
management system.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:11.550
So most of our devices
deal with content--

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:13.135
scan or printed content.

00:25:13.135 --> 00:25:14.760
And the content goes
through the cloud.

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:18.190
And this system basically is
what manages that content.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:21.060
So, in this case, the system is
deployed to data centers, one

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.740
in the US and one in Europe.

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:24.550
And we are basically
using Istio.

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:26.070
So what we are
basically doing here

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:28.680
is we replace Nginx
by the Istio gateway.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.020
So the traffic goes
into the Istio gateway.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:34.020
And then we have Envoy
inject that automatically

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:38.340
by the Istio system in
front of our services.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:40.080
The good thing about
Istio is that Apigee

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:43.330
has integrated this with their
control plane on the cloud.

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.530
So basically, we can have
consistent authentication

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:49.530
and authorization policies
for all of services

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.540
independently of what
is the mechanism we

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.980
use to deploy those services.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:55.700
And that's it.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:56.490
I'll let Nanden--

00:26:03.795 --> 00:26:05.170
NANDEN SRIDHAR:
Thank you, Gallo.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:08.710
So obviously, we can show the
actual systems that HP uses.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:12.580
But we've got a demo that kind
of shows you these technologies

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:15.140
and how they kind
of come together.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:19.630
So in this demo, we
took a simple example

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:21.580
to illustrate the point.

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.850
It's a retail example that
has a bunch of services.

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:25.480
Right?

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:27.250
The first is an order API.

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:31.390
And you have a consumer that's
outside your zone of trust--

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:33.110
could be a mobile
app, online app,

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:37.540
or whatever it is that's making
a request to the order API.

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.700
The order API calls the
inventory microservice.

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:42.850
They're both built
as microservices.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:45.820
And that shows you some
service-to-service connectivity

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:47.800
there, with one of
these services that's

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:49.810
exposed outside.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:52.030
And depending on
certain conditions,

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:54.340
the order API could
also invoke and place

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:57.700
an order on Salesforce
with a partner then.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.170
And that's how they're exposed--

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:02.800
that interfaced
with this order API.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:06.880
So what this example would look
like when actually implemented

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:10.780
with gateways is that you
would have a common ingress

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:13.090
point to this application--

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:16.030
API.retailcompany.com, right?

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:19.105
And your order APIs
are obviously--

00:27:19.105 --> 00:27:22.930
that that's the facade
the request comes to.

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:25.110
And then the
ingress then decides

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:29.470
what to deal with this-- or how
to deal with this API request.

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:31.990
Because this request is
coming from the outside,

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:34.120
you generally would
want to scan the payload

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:38.860
to make sure the JSONs font does
not exceed certain capacity.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:41.260
And you want to do this general
check because it's coming

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:44.110
from a untrusted network.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:46.450
And that's-- what you
see as Message Processor,

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:50.530
that's the name for Apigee's
centralized or enterprise API

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:51.680
gateway.

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:54.970
The gateway then hands it off
to the order microservice.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:58.210
All of these are running in
Kubernetes at this point.

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:00.940
The order microservice
then calls the inventory

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:01.750
microservice.

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.730
But except this
time, it does not

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:06.010
have to go back to that
centralized gateway

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:08.230
even though it's in
the same cluster.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:10.720
It now calls the
microgateway, which

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:13.410
is the only facade the
inventory service exposes--

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:16.180
so there is no way
to bypass the gateway

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:18.830
and go directly to
the inventory service.

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:20.800
And of course,
there's now a call-out

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:24.533
that the order-- now the order
API needs to call Salesforce.

00:28:24.533 --> 00:28:26.200
But obviously I don't
want the order API

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:29.170
to code for Salesforce's
access tokens and all that.

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.060
It's already got an
access token with it.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.850
And that's what it uses
to call Salesforce.

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:36.300
So this time, in the order
API [INAUDIBLE] vendor

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:40.090
API, it calls that
API without knowing

00:28:40.090 --> 00:28:42.550
what this vendor's
authentication authorization

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:43.780
mechanism is.

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:47.860
But the centralized gateway
manages this token mediation

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:51.310
and does this on behalf
of the order API.

00:28:51.310 --> 00:28:52.960
And of course, at
the end of all this,

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:55.000
we are going to see
some analytics to see

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.850
how all these APIs are being
used, who's using them,

00:28:57.850 --> 00:28:59.050
and so on and so forth.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:03.760
All right, so with that,
let's go see the demo.

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:05.560
Demo, please.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:09.060
All right, so I've got a single
Kubernetes cluster on GKE,

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:12.790
where I've got my applications
and microservices installed.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:16.720
And I'm going to
use my CLI to show

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:19.790
you what exactly is running.

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:20.860
Let me go here.

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:27.130
So I'm going to quickly show
you the actual microservices.

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:29.290
So Demo is the
name space I have.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:31.780
And this is where I've got
those two microservices.

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:34.000
I don't know if that's
big enough for you guys.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:35.580
But if you guys can see-- no?

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:37.360
OK, I wish you hadn't said that.

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:41.140
Because I don't know how
to increase the font.

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.340
Sorry, I'll read it out.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:45.030
There are two
microservices there--

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:47.620
the order and the
inventory microservice.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.230
And I know it's hard to
tell, but one of them

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:53.140
is a part with two containers
in it, one of which

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.600
has the microgateway.

00:29:56.600 --> 00:29:59.300
OK, so now that we have seen the
applications that are running,

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:03.710
we'll very quickly show
you the Apigee components

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:07.730
that are running also
on the same gateway.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:11.520
This uses the recently
announced Apigee hybrid,

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.700
which runs the
enterprise gateway

00:30:13.700 --> 00:30:16.230
on any platform of your choice.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:18.080
So this is running now on GKE.

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:20.820
So I've got all my
gateway infrastructure

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:25.190
running in a remote installation
that's now getting instructions

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:27.830
from a central control plane
that's managed by Apigee.

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:29.850
So I've got my Apigee
components running.

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:31.220
It's also got Istio in it.

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:34.950
And we'll see how
they work together.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.190
OK, so now what
I'm going to do is

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:42.290
I'm going to try and
gain access to a part,

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.090
just to kind of
show you to see if I

00:30:44.090 --> 00:30:46.010
can bypass some of
the security systems,

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:48.090
and access these services.

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:50.120
So usually, you would
restrict such access

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:51.380
to Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:54.980
But this time, just to show you
that the services are indeed

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:58.490
protected, I'm going to get
into one of these services.

00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:00.250
I've gotten access
to one of the pods.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:04.500
And now I'm going to try and
access the service directly.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:08.240
So I'm going to call the
inventory service from a part

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:10.340
that's running in Kubernetes.

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:15.680
And if everything works good,
I should get an error message.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:19.040
I get a Missing
Authorization because I'm--

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:20.930
even though I'm
inside the cluster,

00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:22.130
I'm running in another part.

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:23.185
I can't access it.

00:31:23.185 --> 00:31:24.560
Because there's
a sidecar gateway

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:28.760
that's protecting all
of these microservices.

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:35.180
But if I got access to
the inventory service,

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.650
I should then be able
to get access to it.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.650
So now if I've
changed my persona

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:42.530
from being a service operator
who was worried about this

00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:45.860
to being a developer
off the order service

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:48.500
who wants to invoke the
inventory service, what I would

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.920
do is go to the
Developer Portal.

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:55.670
And now, this is a quick example
of the portal that we have.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:57.200
And I go look at
all the services

00:31:57.200 --> 00:31:59.690
that exist in my catalog.

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:01.670
And now you can
control access to what

00:32:01.670 --> 00:32:03.830
microservices I should see.

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:06.500
Internal developers probably
will see most of the catalog.

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:08.880
And external developers may not.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:11.960
And once I have seen the
APIs that I'm interested in,

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:16.460
I could even try accessing
some of these APIs from here,

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:17.820
see how they operate.

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:22.230
So for example, I'm going to
take order as an example here.

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:24.810
Although I am building the
order service, in this example

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:27.500
I'm going to assume that I want
to consume the order service.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:30.230
I'm going to quickly try
and gain some access to it,

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:32.070
look at how the API works.

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:33.410
I see the response.

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:35.680
I get some
documentation about it.

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:37.700
It looks like I'm mostly
good to start building

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:40.530
my application based on this.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.470
But there's one
important step missing.

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:44.780
I never got access to this API.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:46.250
And that's what I'll do next.

00:32:46.250 --> 00:32:46.910
Right?

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:48.660
Now, of course I
pre-created these.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:52.670
But what I would do is go
to my Application Access

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:54.110
and then look at--

00:32:54.110 --> 00:32:57.330
and then request access
to the APIs here.

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:59.780
So here, I already
created, pre-created

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:04.310
this, where I've gotten access
to the order application.

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:06.140
I've got a client
ID and a secret.

00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:08.610
So I can get by
OAR tokens for it.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:12.090
And I can use these tokens
to access my service.

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:14.490
And this is what a typical
developer would do.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:17.480
Now, assuming I've
got this client ID,

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:20.660
now I'm going to try and
access the inventory service

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:24.810
because I now have credentials
to access that service.

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:26.510
So this time, I'm
going to go back

00:33:26.510 --> 00:33:29.180
to the same Kubernetes pod.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:32.540
But this time I'm going
to access it with a key.

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:41.080
So now that I accessed it with
a key, I get a HTTP 200 back.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:45.500
I could use minus-V. And then
you guys will see the response.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:48.520
It's kind of unfortunate that
the font is not big enough.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:50.990
My terminal doesn't
support changing that.

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:52.950
Sorry about that.

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:57.460
OK, so now that we have kind
of protected my east-west

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:58.360
traffic--

00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:01.040
microservice-to-microservice
communication--

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:04.090
let's take a quick look at how
I would protect it for traffic

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:05.650
coming from the outside.

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:10.870
So what I'm going to do next
is very quickly show you

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:15.190
how that setup looks like.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:17.650
If you look at the
containers that I have,

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:20.409
I have the inventory container
and the microgateway container

00:34:20.409 --> 00:34:21.590
in the same pod.

00:34:21.590 --> 00:34:23.909
So the microgateway
will scale and get

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:29.679
upgraded each time I deploy
the pod for inventory.

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:33.370
OK, so now let's go take
a example about trying

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:35.570
to access a partner API.

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:38.500
So in this case, that partner
API would be Salesforce.

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:41.770
I'm not even going to try
and send a parameter to it.

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:46.060
I'll send an empty body to
show you that the partners

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:47.780
API is protected.

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:52.750
So I'm going to send this
empty string to the partner AP.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:54.139
You'll notice two things.

00:34:54.139 --> 00:34:56.560
First, that wasn't a
Salesforce endpoint.

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:57.580
I use Fazio.

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:00.340
Fazio is the name I made
up for the retail service.

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:03.220
So I still use my internal
host name or domain name

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:04.900
to access this API.

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:06.580
The second, it was protected.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:09.160
And so I got an Access
Denied because I didn't

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:11.600
have the tokens to access it.

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:13.840
Now what I would again do
is go back to the portal

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:17.440
and seek access to this API like
any other API in my enterprise

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:21.010
because I don't care what
the partner implemented.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:23.780
So now let me go grab a token.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:27.510
So I'm going to connect
to my token endpoint

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.130
and pass in the client
credentials drawn

00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:32.500
because that's easy to show.

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:36.120
And I'm going to go get
an access token for it.

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:37.870
So now that I've gotten
this access token,

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:42.670
I'm going to try and access
the partner's API now.

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:48.340
And-- let's fix that.

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:06.788
And put the--put the token in.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:19.270
And send in the body.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:40.690
All right, so my
order was submitted.

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:42.980
Now I'm going to quickly
log into Salesforce

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:46.110
and show you that my
order got created.

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:49.580
So if everything worked
fine, I had 15 orders.

00:36:49.580 --> 00:36:51.200
I'm going to do a quick refresh.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:54.620
Hopefully there's a 16th
one that just came in.

00:36:54.620 --> 00:36:58.340
And that was created through
the enterprise gateway

00:36:58.340 --> 00:36:59.990
that I just created now.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:03.020
So there's a quick demo to
show three different use

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:07.610
cases, some east-west traffic
being protected, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:37:07.610 --> 00:37:09.380
and some token mediation.

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:11.300
Thank you all.

